# {{title}}

A [Fynkel](https://fynkel.dev) game — a normal Flutter project plus
`fynkel.project.json`, an `assets/` tree of strict JSON documents and the
Fynkel CLI. Created by `fynkel create`.

```sh
fynkel validate                  # the whole project: documents, references, lock
fynkel test --headless           # the startup scene on the Rust headless host
fynkel test --headless --input test/walk.input.json   # the same with a recorded input stream against the project's actions
flutter test                     # test/game_test.dart: the scene loads, the cube spins, the recording walks it and plays its sounds, the crate lands, the HUD model updates
fynkel run                       # validate, cook, then flutter run with FYNKEL_PROJECT set to this directory
fynkel run -d <iphone>           # the same on a device: the app carries the cooked cache (fynkel_cache/, bundled)
fynkel cook && fynkel render --out render.png   # the scene as a picture, no window
```

Gameplay is Dart: `lib/game/spinner.dart` is a `Behaviour` with `@Expose`
fields that the scene sets; `lib/game/mover.dart` walks the cube with the
`move` action and hops it on `jump` — WASD and Space, a gamepad's left
stick and A button, or the touch overlay on a phone, as
`fynkel.project.json` → `inputActions` binds them (`Input.action2D('move')`,
`Input.pressed('jump')`; docs/input.md in the engine). A behaviour reads
and writes its object's native components too — `get<MeshRenderer>()`,
`set(mesh.copyWith(visible: false))`, `add(const PointLight(…))`,
`remove<SpotLight>()` — and `test/game_test.dart` shows it. Physics is
two more of those components: the Crate has a `fynkel.RigidBody` and a
`fynkel.Collider` and falls onto the Floor (a collider alone); the Cube is
a kinematic body, so walking it pushes the crate. A behaviour applies
forces (`applyImpulse`), reads `bodyState()`, gets `onCollisionEnter` and
casts rays (`world.raycast`). Sound is one more: the Cube's
`fynkel.AudioSource` holds `assets/audio/jump.ogg` (Kenney, CC0) and the
Mover plays it on take-off (`audio.play()`), and `land.ogg` on landing
through `world.audio.play(land)` — an `@Expose`d `AssetRef` the scene
sets; the view opens the output device (`audio: true`) and a test hears
the same on a null backend. Motion that needs no rules at all —
turning, bobbing, following, billboards, timed despawns, spawning at a
rate — is the native catalogue (`fynkel.Rotator`, `Oscillate`, `Follow`,
`LookAtCamera`, `LifetimeDespawn`, `Emitter`): components the runtime
ticks itself, authored in the scene, no Dart. Levels change at runtime —
`world.scenes.transition(id)` loads the next scene at the next frame,
`loadScene(id, mode: LoadMode.additive)` stacks one, `unloadScene` takes
it out — and a `fynkel.TriggerZone` on a sensor names the event a
behaviour or `world.zones.on('collect', …)` gets when something walks in.
The user's settings (volumes, the quality profile Q picks, input rebinds)
live in a `settings.json` under the platform's application-support
directory and are read at launch. What the screen shows lives in
`lib/game/hud_model.dart`, a `GameModel` of observable fields the Mover
writes from `update` and the HUD binds with `ModelBuilder` — a bound
widget rebuilds when a value changed, never per frame — and the labels
over the Cube and the Crate are `WorldLabel`s in a `ProjectionLayer`,
pinned to the objects through the world's projection view (docs/hud.md).
The HUD's stats block is `FynkelFrameStats` (`onStats`, once a second):
where the frame goes — behaviours, native ticks, render(), the GPU's pass
times, the Dart heap's allocation rate on a debug/profile build — with
`FYNKEL_CAPTURE=1` printing it as a `[stats]` line for scripts
(docs/instrumentation.md).
After changing an exposed field, an id or adding a behaviour class, run `fynkel
codegen --project .` and commit the regenerated `lib/generated/` and
`schemas/behaviours/`.

`AGENTS.md` describes the layout, every command and the validate → codegen →
build → test loop for tools and AI agents (and is a good tour for people).

`lib/main.dart` shows the scene in a `FynkelGameView` (`package:fynkel_flutter`:
the 3D viewport and the frame loop) with a Flutter HUD over it; the
renderer reads the cooked cache, so `fynkel run` cooks before it runs (plain
`flutter run` needs a `fynkel cook` first). On a device the project
directory does not exist, so the app opens the copy of the cache it bundles
(`fynkel_cache/`, staged by every cook). `fynkel render` draws the same
scene to a PNG without a window.

`assets/audio/jump.ogg` and `land.ogg` are from Kenney's *Interface
Sounds* and *Impact Sounds* packs (www.kenney.nl), Creative Commons Zero:
free to use, credit optional. Replace them with your own `.wav`/`.ogg`
files; `fynkel cook` imports whatever is in `assets/audio/`.
